David Jao wrote:
> 
> I think wishing for a magic lamp is unbalancing because of the following
> special case of what you just described, which no one has mentioned so
> far:
> 
> After you have used up all the charges in a wand of wishing, you can wrest
> one more charge from the wand, but it typically takes hundreds of turns to
> wring out that wish. If wishing for magic lamps were allowed, I would
> almost always use the last wrest wish on a magic lamp.

That's indeed an interesting and valid point to consider.

On the plus side you could always wish for a magic lamp as soon as the
last ordinary wish from the castle wand is used. On the minus side you
have the higher weight of the lamp compared to the wand, and you have
the unreliability of 80%.

If I would not yet have a magic lamp when finishing the castle level I'd
indeed go for a magic lamp. Though less for a quickly available "last
wish"[*] (which I have with one exception never used in my games anyway)
but because I regard light in Gehennom as so precious.

> The reason is that an 80% chance of a wish that you can obtain in a few
> turns is much more valuable than a 100% chance of a wish that takes
> several hundred turns to wrest.

With my last green pudding incident in mind I agree with you. While in
many real fatal emergency cases two moves (or three with bag management)
for wishing are inacceptable, there are indeed specific situations where
you have an advantage with a quite instant wish (ignoring the 80%).

> This is without even considering the value
> of the magic lamp as a light source.
> 
> IMO the only non-abusive way to allow wishing for magic lamps was if the
> resulting lamp were permanently cursed or some similar unusual game
> balancing mechanism were imposed.

Though I won't agree to your conclusions. I still think that the above
mentioned advantage has not the quality to _significantly_ affect the
*balance* of the game.

Janis

